----------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. THE ROOT CAUSE OF THE DISAGREEMENT
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
The historical rule, read directly from
audit_ide_stubs.ps1.REPAIRED:

    $first = FirstNonBlank $f.FullName
    if ($first -match '^//\s*STUB\b') { $pure.Add(...) }

It inspects ONLY the first non-blank line and requires the literal token STUB
there. It therefore CANNOT SEE:

  * `// Auto-generated stub` -- the regex needs STUB immediately after `//` and
    optional whitespace, and "Auto-generated" is in the way. 153 files.
  * files whose first line is a comment that merely MENTIONS a stub.
  * body-less files with no banner at all.

MEASURED CONSEQUENCE, on src_only|EXT_WITH_HEADER:

    R1 sees                 316
    R2 sees                 492
    R4 sees                 585
    MISSED BY R1            270   (46% of the canonical population)

Sample of the 270 the historical rule cannot see:
    src/agentic_agent_coordinator.cpp
    src/agentic_controller.cpp
    src/agentic_engine.cpp
    src/agentic_loop_state.cpp
    src/agentic_observability.cpp
    src/agent_explainability.cpp
    src/agent_history.cpp
    src/agent_policy.cpp
    src/ai_completion_real.cpp
    src/ai_model_caller.cpp

So the 338/339/417/424/426 spread is NOT measurement noise. It is five
different questions, and the one that produced the most-quoted number used a
rule blind to almost half the population.

The build graph already knows better: rawrxd_filter_missing_sources() applies
R4 plus R5 and declares 56 declared-unimplemented sources. The runtime census
and the build-time census have been answering different questions for months.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. REPRODUCTION ATTEMPT -- AND WHY THE HISTORICAL NUMBERS DO NOT REPRODUCE
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Attempted reproduction of the historical figures with the historical rule R1:

  historical claim                     measured R1        delta
  src stubs, excluding .bak   (339)        316            -23
  tests stubs                 (85)          83             -2
  combined                    (424)        399            -25
  pure_stub_paths.txt         (338)        316            -22
  unattributed               (417)        --          not reproduced
  unattributed               (426)        --          not reproduced

Checked and ruled out as the cause:
  * Directory exclusions are NOT responsible. Re-running R1 with NO exclusions
    gives the same 316, and there are 0 R1 stubs inside build/vendor directories.
    So the counts were not inflated by copies.
  * Extension set is NOT the src/tests gap either; src_only and src_and_tests
    differ from 339/85 by more than the header delta alone.

The actual cause is TREE DRIFT plus MISSING PROVENANCE:

  * STUB_HEADER_REPORT.md -- the source of 339 / 85 / 424 -- IS NOT TRACKED BY
    GIT AT ALL. `git log` on it returns nothing. It records no commit.
  * pure_stub_paths.txt and RAWRXD_STUB_CERTIFICATION_SURFACE_001.md were
    committed in 9f1be7d05, an ancestor of the f66d63e05 baseline.
  * HEAD has since moved again, to e7fb2efa0.

A census that does not record the tree identity it ran against cannot be
reproduced after the tree moves. That is not a flaw in any one script; it is a
missing field.


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
5. CANONICAL NUMBERS
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
Population = TRANSLATION UNITS (.cpp .c .cc .cxx), excluding build output and
vendored code, with no code surviving comment and preprocessor stripping (R4):

    src only                        531
    src + tests                     631
    whole rawrxd tree               673

R5 (trivial `int main(){return 0;}`) contributes 0 additional files under this
definition -- every such file is already counted by R4, which is the correct
relationship and is why the two rules do not double-count.

The canonical population is therefore 531 translation units under src, of which
the historical rule could see 316 (59%) and could not see 215 (41%).


----------------------------------------------------------------------------
6. LEDGER-AUTHORITY VERDICT
----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  CANONICAL_RULE_SET_DEFINED            = YES   (R1..R5, explicit axes)
  HISTORICAL_RULE_BLIND_SPOT            = MEASURED  270 files (46%)
  HISTORICAL_NUMBERS_REPRODUCED         = NO
  HISTORICAL_NUMBERS_EXPLAINED          = PARTIAL
      * the SPREAD is explained (rule blindness + tree drift)
      * the individual VALUES 417 and 426 remain unattributed
  ROOT_CAUSE_OF_IRREPRODUCIBILITY       = census artifacts record no commit
      STUB_HEADER_REPORT_UNTRACKED      = 1  (source of the most-quoted 424)

  GLOBAL_ZERO_IMPACT_CLAIM             = NOT PROVEN, AND NOT PROVABLE FROM THESE
                                          ARTIFACTS. A statement of the form
                                          "comment-only stubs are link-neutral and
                                          zero-impact" is a claim about a
                                          population defined by a rule that sees
                                          59% of it. Retract it as unscoped and
                                          re-derive it under R4 if it is wanted.

  NEXT MEASURE, and it is a small one: record GIT_COMMIT in every census
  artifact. Without it, every future disagreement about stub counts will be
  indistinguishable from ordinary tree drift, and will be argued instead of
  derived.
